; The two shapes a LOCAL rule would get wrong, and the reason the
; analysis is a fixpoint rather than a look at one expression.
;
; `347-result-only-tyvar.ax` has the direct unsound shape (a tail that
; is a `cast`) and `tests/selfhost/976-diverging-tyvar.ax` has the direct
; sound one (a
; tail that never returns). Both are decidable by looking at one
; function. These are not.
;
; `rethrow` and `sneak` have the SAME tail shape as each other - a call
; to another function whose result is a type variable no parameter
; mentions - and opposite truths. `rethrow` is correct, because what it
; calls never returns. `sneak` is unsound, because what it calls
; fabricates a word, and the fabrication reaches the caller through it.
; Nothing about `rethrow`'s own body distinguishes them; only what its
; callee does.
;
; So the set of these functions is solved together: assume they all
; diverge, then strike out any whose tail can produce a value. `sneak`
; goes when `conjure` goes, and `rethrow` survives with `panic`.
;
; `mixed` is the third case a local rule loses: one arm diverges and
; the other fabricates. An `if` diverges only if BOTH arms do, and the
; arm that returns is the one that hands the caller a machine word
; wearing a type it chose.
;
; MEASURED 2026-08-25, on the tree before the analysis existed: all
; five declarations here and in the two files above drew the identical
; AX3040 warning, and `check` exited 0 for every one of them.
;
; Three errors are expected here and `rethrow` must draw none.
(import IO)

(:: panic (-> String a))

(fn (panic msg)
  {
    (writeStr 2 msg)
    (cast a (exit 70))
  }
)

(:: conjure (-> Int a))

(fn (conjure n)
  (cast a n)
)

; CORRECT: diverges, because `panic` does.
(:: rethrow (-> Int a))

(fn (rethrow n) (panic "rethrown\n"))

; UNSOUND: fabricates, because `conjure` does.
(:: sneak (-> Int a))

(fn (sneak n) (conjure n))

; UNSOUND on one arm, which is enough.
(:: mixed (-> Int a))

(fn (mixed n)
  (if (> n 0)
    (panic "big\n")
    (cast a n)
  )
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
